1
00:00:00,100 --> 00:00:02,640
You leave Earth at 99% of the speed of

2
00:00:02,720 --> 00:00:05,660
light. Why does home come back older than

3
00:00:05,740 --> 00:00:05,900
you?

4
00:00:07,100 --> 00:00:08,980
You are gone for one year by your own

5
00:00:09,020 --> 00:00:11,840
watch. You land, and seven years have

6
00:00:11,880 --> 00:00:14,720
passed at home. Nobody cheated. Your watch

7
00:00:14,780 --> 00:00:17,740
is fine. Time itself ran at a different

8
00:00:17,800 --> 00:00:20,400
pace for you. The danger was never the

9
00:00:20,520 --> 00:00:22,780
speed. It was your own clock.

10
00:00:23,680 --> 00:00:26,080
That is not science fiction. Your phone

11
00:00:26,120 --> 00:00:29,020
already depends on it every day. In this

12
00:00:29,060 --> 00:00:31,440
film, I will show you why a moving clock

13
00:00:31,540 --> 00:00:34,300
runs slow, what a trip near light speed

14
00:00:34,340 --> 00:00:37,060
would do to you and the people you leave

15
00:00:37,120 --> 00:00:40,580
behind, and why you can never quite reach

16
00:00:40,620 --> 00:00:41,040
the limit.

17
00:00:42,020 --> 00:00:43,940
Start with the satellites over your head.

18
00:00:44,580 --> 00:00:46,880
Every GPS satellite carries an atomic

19
00:00:46,980 --> 00:00:49,620
clock, and every one of them drifts.

20
00:00:49,940 --> 00:00:52,120
Because they move fast, their clocks run

21
00:00:52,280 --> 00:00:55,240
slow by about seven-millionths of a second

22
00:00:55,300 --> 00:00:58,020
each day. Because they sit higher up in

23
00:00:58,080 --> 00:01:00,960
weaker gravity, they run fast by about

24
00:01:01,000 --> 00:01:03,540
forty-six. The total is about

25
00:01:03,620 --> 00:01:06,020
thirty-eight-millionths of a second a day

26
00:01:06,360 --> 00:01:09,120
gained. That sounds like nothing, but your

27
00:01:09,140 --> 00:01:11,460
position comes from timing light signals.

28
00:01:11,900 --> 00:01:13,780
Leave that drift alone, and the map in

29
00:01:13,820 --> 00:01:15,920
your pocket would wander by around ten

30
00:01:16,000 --> 00:01:18,800
kilometers every day. The system is built

31
00:01:18,840 --> 00:01:21,720
to correct it. Relativity is not a theory

32
00:01:21,820 --> 00:01:24,380
sitting on a shelf. It is in your phone.

33
00:01:25,060 --> 00:01:27,980
In 1971, two physicists bought airline

34
00:01:28,100 --> 00:01:30,480
seats and flew atomic clocks right around

35
00:01:30,500 --> 00:01:32,940
the world, once heading east and once

36
00:01:32,980 --> 00:01:35,440
heading west. When the clocks came home,

37
00:01:35,620 --> 00:01:37,660
they no longer agreed with the clocks that

38
00:01:37,740 --> 00:01:40,500
stayed behind. They were off by billionths

39
00:01:40,560 --> 00:01:42,900
of a second by the amounts the theory

40
00:01:43,000 --> 00:01:45,500
predicted. So the question is not whether

41
00:01:45,560 --> 00:01:48,360
moving clocks run slow. They do. The

42
00:01:48,420 --> 00:01:49,860
question is why.

43
00:01:50,720 --> 00:01:52,620
Here is the rule underneath it all.

44
00:01:53,520 --> 00:01:56,260
Light always passes you at the same speed

45
00:01:56,640 --> 00:01:58,140
however fast you are moving.

46
00:01:59,220 --> 00:02:02,540
Imagine a clock made of light, a flash

47
00:02:02,660 --> 00:02:05,520
bouncing between two mirrors, one tick for

48
00:02:05,580 --> 00:02:06,360
every trip.

49
00:02:07,280 --> 00:02:09,460
Now put that clock on a fast ship and

50
00:02:09,520 --> 00:02:10,680
watch it go past.

51
00:02:11,600 --> 00:02:13,420
From outside, the flash is not going

52
00:02:13,580 --> 00:02:15,620
straight up and down anymore. It is

53
00:02:15,660 --> 00:02:18,760
traveling on a long, slanted path because

54
00:02:18,800 --> 00:02:20,960
the mirrors moved while it was in flight.

55
00:02:21,780 --> 00:02:23,880
The light cannot speed up to cover the

56
00:02:23,920 --> 00:02:26,780
extra distance, so every tick takes

57
00:02:26,840 --> 00:02:30,780
longer. The moving clock runs slow, and so

58
00:02:30,840 --> 00:02:32,960
does everything moving with it: your

59
00:02:33,020 --> 00:02:36,080
heartbeat, your thoughts, your aging.

60
00:02:37,260 --> 00:02:39,880
At 10% of light speed, the slowing is

61
00:02:39,960 --> 00:02:44,120
about half a percent. At 90%, your time

62
00:02:44,220 --> 00:02:46,700
runs at less than half the pace of home.

63
00:02:47,600 --> 00:02:50,540
At 99%, seven to one.

64
00:02:51,400 --> 00:02:55,520
At 99.99%, seventy to one.

65
00:02:56,520 --> 00:02:58,720
You do not need a spaceship to see it.

66
00:02:59,960 --> 00:03:02,160
Cosmic rays hit the top of the atmosphere

67
00:03:02,180 --> 00:03:05,560
and make particles called muons. A muon

68
00:03:05,660 --> 00:03:07,700
lives only about two-millionths of a

69
00:03:07,760 --> 00:03:11,100
second. Even at nearly light speed, that

70
00:03:11,140 --> 00:03:14,660
is enough to cross only about 660 meters.

71
00:03:15,520 --> 00:03:17,920
Yet muons reach the ground in great

72
00:03:18,000 --> 00:03:18,580
numbers.

73
00:03:19,520 --> 00:03:22,500
In 1963, physicists counted them on a

74
00:03:22,560 --> 00:03:25,380
mountain and at sea level, and the numbers

75
00:03:25,440 --> 00:03:27,900
only made sense if the muon's clocks were

76
00:03:27,940 --> 00:03:28,740
running slow.

77
00:03:29,860 --> 00:03:31,780
Several are passing through you as you

78
00:03:31,840 --> 00:03:32,480
watch this.

79
00:03:33,640 --> 00:03:35,580
We make one of these every week.

80
00:03:36,400 --> 00:03:39,020
Subscribing is how the next one finds you.

81
00:03:40,120 --> 00:03:42,800
Now take that slow clock and put yourself

82
00:03:42,840 --> 00:03:43,580
inside it.

83
00:03:44,440 --> 00:03:47,100
You and your twin say goodbye. You board a

84
00:03:47,140 --> 00:03:50,880
ship that reaches 99% of light speed, fly

85
00:03:50,920 --> 00:03:53,440
to a star ten light-years away, turn

86
00:03:53,500 --> 00:03:55,360
around, and come straight back.

87
00:03:56,240 --> 00:03:58,420
On Earth, your twin waits a little over

88
00:03:58,480 --> 00:04:01,980
twenty years. On board, about three years

89
00:04:02,040 --> 00:04:02,780
pass for you.

90
00:04:03,760 --> 00:04:05,720
You step off the ship three years older.

91
00:04:06,180 --> 00:04:09,560
Your twin is twenty years older. The trip

92
00:04:09,660 --> 00:04:12,480
did not damage you. It simply spent less

93
00:04:12,520 --> 00:04:15,060
of your time. From inside the ship,

94
00:04:15,200 --> 00:04:18,120
nothing feels slow. Your coffee cools at

95
00:04:18,140 --> 00:04:20,740
the normal rate. So what would you see out

96
00:04:20,800 --> 00:04:23,640
of the window? The stars ahead crowd

97
00:04:23,680 --> 00:04:26,340
together and shift towards blue. The stars

98
00:04:26,380 --> 00:04:29,560
behind thin out and fade to red. And

99
00:04:29,620 --> 00:04:32,400
something else changes, the distance.

100
00:04:33,300 --> 00:04:35,420
Ahead of you, the gap to that star

101
00:04:35,560 --> 00:04:38,520
shrinks. Ten light-years of space becomes

102
00:04:38,580 --> 00:04:41,160
about one and a half. That is how you

103
00:04:41,200 --> 00:04:43,720
cross it in so little of your own time

104
00:04:44,140 --> 00:04:46,340
without ever passing the speed of light.

105
00:04:47,460 --> 00:04:50,500
But wait. From your seat, it was Earth

106
00:04:50,540 --> 00:04:53,220
that was moving. Why isn't your twin the

107
00:04:53,260 --> 00:04:53,920
younger one?

108
00:04:54,900 --> 00:04:56,920
Because the two trips were not the same.

109
00:04:57,620 --> 00:05:00,660
Your twin never changed course. You fired

110
00:05:00,700 --> 00:05:03,920
your engines, stopped, turned around, and

111
00:05:04,000 --> 00:05:07,140
changed direction. In that turn, you

112
00:05:07,200 --> 00:05:09,360
switched from one frame of motion to

113
00:05:09,400 --> 00:05:12,200
another, and your twin never did.

114
00:05:13,220 --> 00:05:15,920
The paradox breaks there. The one who

115
00:05:15,980 --> 00:05:18,220
turns around comes home younger.

116
00:05:19,520 --> 00:05:21,440
The clocks on the airliners settled the

117
00:05:21,480 --> 00:05:23,340
same question with real hardware.

118
00:05:24,480 --> 00:05:26,740
So why haven't we done it? Because going

119
00:05:26,920 --> 00:05:30,120
fast costs more than it seems. Take a

120
00:05:30,180 --> 00:05:32,740
single ton, about the mass of a small car.

121
00:05:33,440 --> 00:05:35,960
To push it to 90% of light speed takes

122
00:05:36,020 --> 00:05:38,620
around a fifth of all the energy humanity

123
00:05:38,780 --> 00:05:41,920
uses in a year. To push the same ton from

124
00:05:42,160 --> 00:05:46,080
90 to 99% takes nearly five times as much,

125
00:05:46,480 --> 00:05:48,420
close to a whole year of the world's

126
00:05:48,480 --> 00:05:52,900
energy for one small car. Each extra nine

127
00:05:53,020 --> 00:05:55,820
costs more than the last. Closer to the

128
00:05:55,880 --> 00:05:58,400
limit, the energy needed grows without

129
00:05:58,500 --> 00:06:01,700
end. That is why nothing with mass ever

130
00:06:01,740 --> 00:06:04,700
reaches light speed. It is not a wall you

131
00:06:04,760 --> 00:06:07,500
hit. It is a hill that keeps getting

132
00:06:07,700 --> 00:06:11,464
steeper. And at those speeds, space itself

133
00:06:11,604 --> 00:06:14,904
turns hard. Every speck of interstellar

134
00:06:15,024 --> 00:06:17,624
dust becomes a tiny explosion against the

135
00:06:17,664 --> 00:06:20,744
front of the ship. For scale, the fastest

136
00:06:20,784 --> 00:06:23,104
thing we have ever built is the probe that

137
00:06:23,164 --> 00:06:26,584
skims past the sun. It reaches about six

138
00:06:26,624 --> 00:06:28,984
hundredths of one percent of light speed.

139
00:06:29,584 --> 00:06:32,204
Plans exist to push tiny chips lighter

140
00:06:32,224 --> 00:06:34,784
than a paperclip to a fifth of light speed

141
00:06:34,824 --> 00:06:38,324
with lasers. Nothing human-sized is close.

142
00:06:39,224 --> 00:06:41,644
Here is the strange gift inside all this.

143
00:06:42,484 --> 00:06:45,364
A ship that could keep accelerating at one

144
00:06:45,564 --> 00:06:48,884
Earth gravity, then turn and slow down for

145
00:06:48,924 --> 00:06:51,424
the second half, could cross the whole

146
00:06:51,524 --> 00:06:54,724
Milky Way in a little over twenty years of

147
00:06:54,764 --> 00:06:55,584
the crew's time.

148
00:06:56,604 --> 00:06:59,604
A human lifetime is enough to reach almost

149
00:06:59,684 --> 00:07:03,544
anywhere. But on Earth, a hundred thousand

150
00:07:03,644 --> 00:07:07,204
years would pass. Everyone you knew and

151
00:07:07,364 --> 00:07:09,884
everyone who remembered them would be gone

152
00:07:10,164 --> 00:07:12,904
long before you arrived. You could not

153
00:07:12,964 --> 00:07:16,004
come back to the home you left, only to a

154
00:07:16,064 --> 00:07:18,104
home that had kept going without you.

155
00:07:19,044 --> 00:07:21,484
That is the familiar danger hidden in the

156
00:07:21,524 --> 00:07:21,944
title.

157
00:07:22,904 --> 00:07:25,784
It is not the speed. It is your own clock

158
00:07:26,224 --> 00:07:29,264
drifting away from everyone else's. You

159
00:07:29,364 --> 00:07:32,044
already live inside this. Your phone

160
00:07:32,124 --> 00:07:35,464
depends on it every day. A muon carries it

161
00:07:35,504 --> 00:07:37,844
through your body as you sit here. The

162
00:07:37,904 --> 00:07:40,104
light from the screen in front of you is

163
00:07:40,164 --> 00:07:42,764
traveling at the one speed every clock

164
00:07:42,904 --> 00:07:43,584
agrees on.

165
00:07:44,984 --> 00:07:47,344
What if the speed limit of the universe is

166
00:07:47,384 --> 00:07:51,164
not a wall at all, but a trade? The faster

167
00:07:51,244 --> 00:07:54,324
you go, the more of everyone else's future

168
00:07:54,464 --> 00:07:55,204
you spend.

169
00:07:56,184 --> 00:07:58,404
The bigger question is where you would

170
00:07:58,464 --> 00:08:01,804
choose to land if you knew home would not

171
00:08:01,864 --> 00:08:02,524
be waiting.

172
00:08:04,444 --> 00:08:07,544
Next week, could a robot survive on Mars?
